body:
WHAT
brain/ already holds every model-facing text (brain-is-the-single-source). This feature adds the second half: the engine's behaviour knobs. A new brain/behavior.json, validated by tools/brain/compile.mjs and compiled into the engine like the rest of brain/, holds every hard-coded behaviour policy value that shapes how the agent works. That covers:
- the finishing ladder: each rung on or off, the shared nudge budget, the cutoff streak, the wrap-up thresholds;
- the stall detector's repeat threshold and pivot count;
- clarify's question cap;
- the silent-reasoning threshold;
- overflow recoveries and the default tool-output byte limit;
- the plan-first reminder;
- OVERDRIVE's own behaviour beyond its prompt section: self-verify on or off, the pre-turn snapshot, and how each guard acts while OVERDRIVE is on;
- the detector lists the evidence rungs judge by: test-double markers, browser-run markers, code and UI file extensions.
Each shipped value equals the constant it replaces, so the default brain behaves exactly as before. Changing a value and running npm run build changes that behaviour. The compiler rejects unknown keys, wrong types and out-of-range values, naming the key. The remaining instructional texts still composed in code also move into brain/prompts with byte-identical rendering:
- the steering notes engine.ts sends with user-attached images;
- the Monitor noise-stop and background-task notifications.
subagent.* prompts and engine/core/src/agent/agents.ts stay out of scope. Safety floors stay in code: deny rules always refuse, and a tool_use always gets its tool_result.

WHY
The owner wants to specialize MAGENTRA by editing brain/ alone: how it describes itself, how it behaves in OVERDRIVE, how hard it pushes before ending a turn. Prose alone cannot do that, because the rules for when prose fires and what the harness does are constants in session.ts, finishing.ts and permissions.ts.

WHERE
brain/behavior.json and brain/README.md (new section); tools/brain/compile.mjs (schema and validation); engine/protocol/src/brain.ts (typed accessor); engine/core/src/runtime/session.ts, finishing.ts, permissions.ts, engine.ts and engine/tools/src where a knob is read; BIG-PICTURE §5, §6, §7 and §12.

TEST CHECKLIST
1. Every key in brain/behavior.json is read by the engine, and every replaced constant is gone from code: no knob is defined twice.
2. With the shipped behavior.json, scripted turns that exercise each rung, the stall detector, clarify and OVERDRIVE send byte-identical provider requests and events to the build before this feature.
3. Changing a representative knob in a temp brain changes the behaviour: a rung switched off does not fire; a lower nudge budget stops sooner; OVERDRIVE self-verify off does not run the silent round.
4. The compiler rejects an unknown key, a wrong type and an out-of-range value, naming the key.
5. Every moved instructional text renders byte-identically to its old literal.
6. The existing suite, test:ui and the pins stay green with no assertion changed.
